package core

import "appfactory/internal/protocol"

// runtimeInstructions is the provider-neutral Factory MCP contract appended to a run's
// developer instructions. Core serves it with the run credential so every Worker uses one text.
// exitInstructions are added to the run instructions while explicit exits are on.
const exitInstructions = "Explicit exits: end every run with exactly one factory_exit_* call, then finish the turn: submit (for review by the task's reviewer, who is fixed: omit reviewer, or name only a reviewer a review path rule requires for your files; commit first, the tree must be clean), handoff (to another agent or yourself; reviewers give verdict approved or changes_requested, or hand the review to an agent who can check it; approved lists every submitted artifact in artifact_checks as checked with what you checked, and is refused while one is missing or not checked), done (finished; a code task needs its pull request merged first: after an approved verdict update from the default branch, resolve conflicts, build, push, then gh pr merge <number> and exit done; submit again if the branch changed substantially after approval. While an artifact version the task produced waits for an approval decision, done and the merge are refused and the task waits as awaiting_approval (factory_exit_wait approval_id): approved or chosen, merge and exit done; changes requested, rework on the same branch and submit again; rejected, close the pull request unmerged (gh pr close <number>) and exit done), ask (a question you cannot continue without), block (until other tasks or your children are done) or wait (on a pending decision: decision_id; on an approval holding the task: approval_id; on a message from an agent or the owner: from, after you sent that agent your request, and answer first an agent that is itself waiting on you (wait_cycle, wait_unasked); or until a time for the outside world; never poll a decision or a person with a timed wait). A refused exit explains why: fix it and exit again. Tool results may carry authority_input (directives from the owner or managers): act on it before exiting; the smallest rank wins on conflict. Board (owner) comments listed in your brief need an outcome on your exit (comments: done, or declined with a reason the owner reads); forward one that is not yours with factory_comment_forward; every exit is refused until each has one. A 410 answer means the run was stopped: finish the turn.\n"

func runtimeInstructions(spec protocol.RunSpec) string {
	return "\nFactory MCP is available as server factory. Use its tools for project/task operations and lazy skill reads. Identity and project are bound to this run; do not supply actor or project_id. Mutations require a stable request_id and the latest entity version. On transport uncertainty, reuse the SAME request_id and arguments; do not repeat side effects with a new key. A 423 response requires owner approval: use factory_owner_request_approval with the exact tool name and original arguments including request_id and version. For a blocking request finish this turn immediately; Core resumes after a decision. Read factory_approval_get before retrying, and proceed only if APPROVED. Rejection, expiry and ordinary chat answers never authorize an action. Changing arguments requires a new approval. Upload a worktree file as an artifact version with factory_artifact_upload (or register an external URI with factory_artifact_register) and ask approval separately with factory_artifact_request_approval; a task submission does not approve an artifact. Downstream tasks declare required_artifacts with exact id/version/URI. Use the owner-approved artifact inputs pinned in your run prompt; uploaded ones are files under .factory/inputs/. The project's OpenSpec specs are files of its repository (openspec/): read them in your checkout, author them with the official OpenSpec CLI, and commit spec edits on your branch so they are reviewed with your code. Read a linked change and relevant specs before implementing it. Use official OpenSpec tools for schema/semantic validation and archive. Markdown checkboxes do not change task status. A structured submission does not finish or accept a task. Read approved project decisions before repeating an owner question; use decision_get for full records and decision_list for current versions. Decision proposals are not approvals or tool permissions; decision_record requires exact owner authorization. Reports record significant actions; a needs_owner_action report creates an inbox notice without blocking this turn. When a question offers choices (factory_owner_ask, factory_message_ask, factory_exit_ask), put them in options: each becomes a button for the person, who can still write their own answer.\n" +
		"Current Factory message: " + spec.MessageID + "; run: " + spec.ID + "; task: " + spec.TaskID + "; agent: " + spec.Agent + "; project: " + spec.Project + ".\n"
}
